actually a property of the physical system itself.

760
00:35:25,780 --> 00:35:26,040
Right.

761
00:35:26,040 --> 00:35:29,140
It's a property of the math we are using to look at the system.

762
00:35:29,340 --> 00:35:32,580
You are grasping the exact magnitude of what this text proves.

763
00:35:33,060 --> 00:35:37,100
Entanglement is not an intrinsic, unchangeable property of a physical matrix.

764
00:35:37,300 --> 00:35:38,200
It is just, wow.

765
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:39,380
It is field relative.

766
00:35:39,720 --> 00:35:43,440
It is entirely relative to the scalar field, real or complex, doing the composing.

767
00:35:43,720 --> 00:35:48,680
Because if you switch to complex numbers, that invisible J tensor J witness is no longer

768
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:51,740
built out of locally impossible antisymmetric matrices.

769
00:35:52,060 --> 00:35:52,260
Right.

770
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:54,320
Because complex numbers handle that inherently.

771
00:35:54,520 --> 00:35:55,020
Exactly.

772
00:35:55,020 --> 00:36:01,460
In complex numbers, it is just negative one times the normal product of complex polymatrices.

773
00:36:01,820 --> 00:36:05,100
It's totally observable using complex local bases.

774
00:36:05,560 --> 00:36:12,460
What requires spooky entanglement in real numbers is just a mundane classical correlation in complex

775
00:36:12,460 --> 00:36:12,880
numbers.

776
00:36:13,080 --> 00:36:14,140
That is mind-blowing.

777
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:15,520
I need to put this into an analogy.

778
00:36:16,200 --> 00:36:19,480
Imagine two people sitting in a room sharing a secret language.

779
00:36:19,640 --> 00:36:19,840
Right.

780
00:36:20,040 --> 00:36:24,700
Let's say they are perfectly fluent in Navajo and they are discussing a complex engineering

781
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:25,160
project.

782
00:36:25,260 --> 00:36:25,480
Okay.

783
00:36:25,560 --> 00:36:26,000
I'm tracking.

784
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:29,080
Now, imagine an English-only speaker is watching them.

785
00:36:29,480 --> 00:36:32,220
The English speaker represents real numbers.

786
00:36:32,680 --> 00:36:36,500
To the English speaker, these two people seem telepathically linked.

787
00:36:36,580 --> 00:36:36,760
Right.

788
00:36:36,800 --> 00:36:38,220
Because they can't understand the medium.

789
00:36:38,220 --> 00:36:38,740
Exactly.

790
00:36:39,160 --> 00:36:43,440
They are coordinating these incredibly complex actions, handing each other tools, anticipating

791
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:47,060
moves, without saying a single word the English speaker can comprehend.

792
00:36:47,780 --> 00:36:50,080
To the English speaker, they are entangled.

793
00:36:50,420 --> 00:36:51,120
It's spooky action.

794
00:36:51,700 --> 00:36:54,700
But what happens when a bilingual speaker walks into the room?

795
00:36:54,960 --> 00:36:57,480
The bilingual speaker represents complex numbers.

796
00:36:57,480 --> 00:37:02,340
They walk in, they listen for two seconds, and the illusion completely shatters.

797
00:37:02,520 --> 00:37:03,340
It all becomes clear.

798
00:37:03,520 --> 00:37:06,840
The bilingual speaker realizes, oh, they aren't telepathic.

799
00:37:06,980 --> 00:37:08,180
They aren't magically entangled.

800
00:37:08,460 --> 00:37:11,180
They are just reading from the exact same translated script.

801
00:37:11,320 --> 00:37:13,720
They're just speaking a language with a richer vocabulary.

802
00:37:14,020 --> 00:37:14,340
Yes.

803
00:37:14,620 --> 00:37:19,420
The entanglement is an illusion caused entirely by the English speaker's limited grammar.

804
00:37:19,760 --> 00:37:22,400
It's totally separable once you have the right vocabulary.

805
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:27,120
It forces us to take a massive step back and question absolutely everything.

806
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:32,640
How much of quantum weirdness is a profound, inescapable truth about reality?

807
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:37,520
And how much of it is just a shadow cast by our choice of mathematical notation?

808
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:39,900
Which brings us to the assumption audit.

809
00:37:40,400 --> 00:37:45,600
Because if quantum mechanics is so heavily dependent on the mathematical grammar we choose

810
00:37:45,600 --> 00:37:50,840
to use, what do we actually have to assume to build the standard, complex quantum theory

811
00:37:50,840 --> 00:37:51,640
we use today?

812
00:37:51,640 --> 00:37:58,260
After 78 pages of uncompromising dense derivation, the author tallies up the final bill.

813
00:37:58,900 --> 00:38:05,180
Out of all the possible ways to build a universe, the author identifies exactly four independent

814
00:38:05,180 --> 00:38:08,700
physical assumptions used to recover standard quantum mechanics.

815
00:38:08,840 --> 00:38:09,140
Four.

816
00:38:09,460 --> 00:38:12,260
Just four pillars holding up the entire sky.

817
00:38:12,380 --> 00:38:12,920
Only four.

818
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:14,540
Let's walk through them one by one.

819
00:38:14,820 --> 00:38:15,300
Number one.

820
00:38:15,820 --> 00:38:16,960
Local tomography.

821
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:22,440
The assumption we just spent all that time discussing, the idea that joint states are

822
00:38:22,440 --> 00:38:24,540
fully described by local measurements.

823
00:38:24,680 --> 00:38:24,900
Right.

824
00:38:25,100 --> 00:38:27,100
Remember, the author didn't derive this as a given.

825
00:38:27,520 --> 00:38:32,100
They assumed it, tested it, and found that complex numbers pass the test perfectly.

826
00:38:32,240 --> 00:38:32,400
Right.

827
00:38:32,500 --> 00:38:35,040
The 16 equals 16 dimension match we did earlier.

828
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:35,940
That's pillar one.

829
00:38:36,040 --> 00:38:36,760
What is number two?

830
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:37,280
Two.

831
00:38:37,900 --> 00:38:40,040
Pure states are frame-completable vectors.

832
00:38:40,280 --> 00:38:41,220
Frame-completable vectors.

833
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:42,700
Let's translate that for everyone.

834
00:38:42,700 --> 00:38:47,420
In simple terms, it means that if you have a state of absolute certainty, a pure state,

835
00:38:47,540 --> 00:38:51,960
where you know exactly what the system is doing, you can mathematically treat that state

836
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:54,880
as just one column in a larger transition matrix.

837
00:38:54,980 --> 00:38:56,620
You can complete the frame around it.

838
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:57,420
Exactly.

839
00:38:57,780 --> 00:39:00,600
You complete the frame to map out all the other possibilities.

840
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:02,860
And what does assuming that actually buy us?

841
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:03,940
It buys us everything.

842
00:39:04,520 --> 00:39:09,000
By making this one geometric assumption, the author instantly derives the Born Rule.

843
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:14,920
The Born Rule is the fundamental law that tells us how to calculate the probability of a quantum event happening.

844
00:39:15,060 --> 00:39:15,280
Right.

845
00:39:15,560 --> 00:39:16,720
You square the amplitude.

846
00:39:17,100 --> 00:39:17,480
Exactly.

847
00:39:17,960 --> 00:39:21,400
In most textbooks, the Born Rule is just handed to you as a postulate.

848
00:39:21,800 --> 00:39:23,000
This is how probability works.

849
00:39:23,180 --> 00:39:23,860
Don't ask why.

850
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:24,820
Just accept it.

851
00:39:24,920 --> 00:39:25,100
Right.

852
00:39:25,580 --> 00:39:33,280
But here, the Born Rule emerges organically just from assuming that pure states can be placed inside a complete mathematical frame.

853
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:34,500
That's incredibly elegant.

854
00:39:35,160 --> 00:39:35,360
Okay.

855
00:39:35,560 --> 00:39:36,340
Pillar number three.

856
00:39:36,340 --> 00:39:36,900
Three.

857
00:39:36,900 --> 00:39:40,540
Measurement outcomes are labeled by real numbers.

858
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:41,020
Okay.

859
00:39:41,100 --> 00:39:42,280
That feels incredibly obvious.

860
00:39:42,760 --> 00:39:46,440
If I measure the weight of a quantum particle, my digital scale gives me a real number.

861
00:39:46,560 --> 00:39:47,760
It says 5.2.

862
00:39:47,980 --> 00:39:49,600
It doesn't give me an imaginary number.

863
00:39:49,720 --> 00:39:54,960
It seems obvious to our human experience, but mathematically, this is a profound choice.

864
00:39:55,220 --> 00:39:55,600
How so?

865
00:39:55,800 --> 00:40:01,600
This specific assumption is what forces quantum observables to be described by Hermitian matrices.

866
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:10,120
It forces the math to ensure that the eigenvalues, the actual readings on your digital scale, are strictly real numbers.

867
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:10,880
Ah.

868
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:11,220
Okay.

869
00:40:11,220 --> 00:40:14,620
It's a choice about how we label the reality we interact with.

870
00:40:14,740 --> 00:40:16,660
And finally, pillar number four.

871
00:40:17,060 --> 00:40:17,380
Four.

872
00:40:17,960 --> 00:40:21,740
Time evolution is a strongly continuous unitary group.

873
00:40:21,740 --> 00:40:24,100
Let me focus on the word continuous there.

874
00:40:24,780 --> 00:40:26,180
Time flows smoothly.

875
00:40:27,100 --> 00:40:32,240
The universe doesn't tick forward in discrete, chunky frames like a 60 frames per second video game.

876
00:40:32,360 --> 00:40:32,620
Yes.

877
00:40:32,900 --> 00:40:34,440
Time is smooth and reversible.

878
00:40:34,900 --> 00:40:41,280
And from that assumption of smooth, continuous evolution, the author derives the Schrodinger equation.

879
00:40:41,440 --> 00:40:42,000
Just from that.

880
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:45,840
And they do it just from a simple, finite-dimensional, ordinary differential equation.

881
00:40:45,940 --> 00:40:50,760
They don't even need the heavy, infinite-dimensional math usually required in textbooks, like Stone's theorem.

882
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:51,700
I have to pause here.

883
00:40:51,900 --> 00:40:52,540
That's really it.

884
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:53,300
Just four rules.

885
00:40:53,420 --> 00:40:53,760
That's it.

886
00:40:53,980 --> 00:40:55,300
You assume local tomography.

887
00:40:55,940 --> 00:40:58,280
You assume states can be completed into frames.

888
00:40:58,580 --> 00:41:00,460
You use real numbers for your dials.

889
00:41:00,780 --> 00:41:02,460
And you assume time flows smoothly.

890
00:41:03,020 --> 00:41:08,820
And from those four seeds, you grow the entire massive, complex tree of quantum mechanics.

891
00:41:08,900 --> 00:41:09,320
You do.

892
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:10,480
It scales perfectly.

893
00:41:10,480 --> 00:41:20,300
Even highly complex fuzzy measurements, known in physics as POVMs or positive operator-valued measures, emerge naturally.

894
00:41:20,500 --> 00:41:22,060
How do fuzzy measurements fit in?

895
00:41:22,260 --> 00:41:25,520
The text uses a mathematical trick called the Neumark dilation.

896
00:41:25,640 --> 00:41:27,280
What does a Neumark dilation do?

897
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:32,620
Imagine you are trying to measure a quantum system, but your ruler is blurry.

898
00:41:32,960 --> 00:41:34,480
It's a fuzzy, generalized measurement.

899
00:41:35,240 --> 00:41:36,420
Okay, I've got a blurry ruler.

900
00:41:36,420 --> 00:41:43,500
The Neumark dilation proves that any fuzzy measurement is actually just a perfectly sharp, clean measurement happening on a larger system.

901
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:44,420
Oh, that's wild.

902
00:41:44,560 --> 00:41:49,620
You just couple your little system to a larger environment, evolve them smoothly together, and then measure the whole thing sharply.

903
00:41:49,920 --> 00:41:53,540
Everything we observe in quantum labs scales perfectly from those four original seeds.

904
00:41:53,540 --> 00:42:03,320
On one hand, four is an astonishingly small number of assumptions to generate the most successful, most heavily tested physical theory in human history.

905
00:42:03,560 --> 00:42:04,440
It really is.

906
00:42:04,440 --> 00:42:08,780
But, on the other hand, it proves that quantum mechanics isn't inevitable.

907
00:42:09,260 --> 00:42:11,560
It's not the only way the universe could have been programmed.

908
00:42:12,180 --> 00:42:13,560
It is a specific route.

909
00:42:14,040 --> 00:42:17,820
We chose those four assumptions based on how we interact with the world.

910
00:42:18,080 --> 00:42:21,880
Which sets us up perfectly for the massive plot twist in the final chapters.

911
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:23,140
Yes, the plot twist.

912
00:42:23,140 --> 00:42:33,980
Just when the book has thoroughly convinced us that complex numbers are absolutely mandatory because they are the only field that passes the local tomography dimension counting test perfectly.

913
00:42:34,160 --> 00:42:35,960
The author pulls the rug out from under us.

914
00:42:36,020 --> 00:42:37,000
A total rug pull.

915
00:42:37,480 --> 00:42:44,120
Because they cite groundbreaking new research published right around the same time in 2025 and 2026.

916
00:42:44,520 --> 00:42:45,640
Let's talk about those papers.

917
00:42:45,880 --> 00:42:51,100
There are papers by Haforman and Woods and another major paper by Barrios Hita and colleagues.

918
00:42:51,100 --> 00:42:55,520
These papers attack the very foundation of the local tomography dimension counting.

919
00:42:56,040 --> 00:42:56,060
Okay.

920
00:42:56,220 --> 00:42:59,160
They target the mathematical grammar we use to count the parameters.

921
00:42:59,480 --> 00:43:01,240
They target the Kronecker product.

922
00:43:01,560 --> 00:43:02,500
The Kronecker product.

923
00:43:02,820 --> 00:43:03,080
Okay.

924
00:43:03,260 --> 00:43:03,760
Let me review.

925
00:43:04,380 --> 00:43:13,460
When we were counting dimensions earlier, when we said four parameters times four parameters equals 16 for complex numbers, or three times three equals nine for real numbers,

926
00:43:13,700 --> 00:43:20,200
we assumed a specific mathematical rule for how you multiply those matrices together to combine the systems.

927
00:43:20,200 --> 00:43:20,620
Yes.

928
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:22,900
That rule is the Kronecker product.

929
00:43:23,240 --> 00:43:25,180
We assumed it because it is standard.

930
00:43:25,420 --> 00:43:27,660
It is the default grammar of quantum mechanics.

931
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:29,060
But it is an assumption.

932
00:43:29,380 --> 00:43:30,500
It's a choice of representation.

933
00:43:30,940 --> 00:43:31,220
Okay.

934
00:43:31,500 --> 00:43:36,280
What these 2025 and 2026 papers show is that if you change the composition rule,

935
00:43:37,100 --> 00:43:40,740
you change the mathematical grammar of how two systems combine on paper,

936
00:43:41,520 --> 00:43:44,640
you can completely circumvent the failure of real numbers.

937
00:43:44,860 --> 00:43:46,460
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

938
00:43:46,540 --> 00:43:47,740
You don't change the physics.

939
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:49,140
You don't change the particles.

940
00:43:49,140 --> 00:43:49,460
No.

941
00:43:49,460 --> 00:43:53,180
You just change the arithmetic rule for how you multiply the matrices on your chalkboard.

942
00:43:53,260 --> 00:43:53,700
Exactly.

943
00:43:53,700 --> 00:43:58,260
Specifically, they introduce a different tensor product, denoted with a little r,

944
00:43:58,260 --> 00:44:01,260
that mathematically quotients out what's known as phase redistribution.

945
00:44:02,060 --> 00:44:03,420
Let's break down phase redistribution.

946
00:44:03,820 --> 00:44:09,960
In complex quantum mechanics, a global phase, a sort of overall mathematical shift in the wave,

947
00:44:10,100 --> 00:44:16,600
can be arbitrarily assigned to particle A or particle B without changing any of the observable physics.

948
00:44:16,600 --> 00:44:17,040
Right.

949
00:44:17,160 --> 00:44:17,780
It's ambiguous.

950
00:44:18,060 --> 00:44:23,640
The standard Kronecker product, when forced to use real numbers, gets horribly tangled up by this ambiguity.

951
00:44:23,980 --> 00:44:27,200
It creates phantom mathematical dimensions to account for it,

952
00:44:27,240 --> 00:44:31,040
which is exactly why we ended up with 10 dimensions instead of 9 earlier.

953
00:44:31,320 --> 00:44:34,380
The grammar was creating dimensions that didn't physically exist.

954
00:44:34,380 --> 00:44:35,980
It's like the new grammar fixes it.

955
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:41,920
If you quotient out that ambiguity mathematically, if you tell the math to ignore that phase shift,

956
00:44:42,120 --> 00:44:45,680
you can reproduce all of quantum mechanics using only real numbers.

957
00:44:45,840 --> 00:44:46,140
All of it.

958
00:44:46,400 --> 00:44:53,120
Every single empirical prediction, every experiment, replicated perfectly without a single imaginary eye.

959
00:44:53,120 --> 00:44:57,480
So wait, we can build the universe with real numbers after all.

960
00:44:57,640 --> 00:44:58,280
We can.

961
00:44:58,740 --> 00:45:01,540
If that's true, then why on earth do we use complex numbers?

962
00:45:01,980 --> 00:45:05,360
Where does the imaginary eye come from if reality doesn't actually need it?

963
00:45:05,500 --> 00:45:07,500
This is perhaps my favorite part of the entire text.

964
00:45:07,600 --> 00:45:09,040
This is where we talk about the amplitwist.

965
00:45:09,340 --> 00:45:10,460
The amplitwist.

966
00:45:10,900 --> 00:45:13,160
That sounds like a 1960s dance move.

967
00:45:13,360 --> 00:45:18,380
It does, but the author cites Tristan Needham's work on visual complex analysis.

968
00:45:19,260 --> 00:45:20,760
What is a complex number, really?

969
00:45:20,760 --> 00:45:22,900
We get so hung up on the word imaginary.

970
00:45:23,220 --> 00:45:24,020
It sounds so mystical.

971
00:45:24,420 --> 00:45:27,980
But multiplication by eye isn't some mystical ghostly process.

972
00:45:28,520 --> 00:45:31,900
Geometrically, it is simply simultaneous scaling and rotation.

973
00:45:32,340 --> 00:45:33,540
It is an amplitwist.

974
00:45:33,660 --> 00:45:34,720
Scaling and rotation.

975
00:45:35,340 --> 00:45:36,940
Stretching a vector and twisting it.

976
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:38,880
Think back to the invisible matrix J.

977
00:45:39,460 --> 00:45:45,620
The quarter turn, 90 degree rotation matrix that was hiding inside the invisible real dimension J tensor J.

978
00:45:45,780 --> 00:45:47,440
Yeah, the one that acts exactly like eye,

979
00:45:47,440 --> 00:45:51,280
because rotating 90 degrees twice points you backward to negative one.

980
00:45:51,600 --> 00:45:53,960
The text proves that J literally is eye.

981
00:45:54,280 --> 00:45:57,860
They are identical geometric operations just written in different fonts.

982
00:45:58,080 --> 00:45:58,300
Wow.

983
00:45:58,420 --> 00:46:00,540
Complex numbers are not magical entities.

984
00:46:00,900 --> 00:46:06,880
They are just a highly efficient compressed bookkeeping tool for real-world geometric twists and rotations in a plane.

985
00:46:07,080 --> 00:46:09,960
Here is where it gets incredibly profound to me.

986
00:46:09,960 --> 00:46:14,120
The author isn't invalidating their own earlier proofs.

987
00:46:14,220 --> 00:46:14,920
No, not at all.

988
00:46:15,080 --> 00:46:18,020
They prove that real numbers fail under the Kronecker representation.

989
00:46:18,720 --> 00:46:22,980
But by showing that changing the grammar fixes the problem, they prove something deeper.

990
00:46:23,700 --> 00:46:27,360
Complex numbers aren't a mystical requirement handed down by the universe.

991
00:46:27,500 --> 00:46:27,940
Exactly.

992
00:46:28,100 --> 00:46:33,680
They are just the most efficient human language for describing a universe that involves geometric twists.

993
00:46:33,680 --> 00:46:34,440
Precisely.

994
00:46:34,840 --> 00:46:37,200
The ambiguity isn't destroyed by the new math.

995
00:46:37,440 --> 00:46:38,660
It's just reorganized.

996
00:46:39,080 --> 00:46:42,840
The structure of reality is perfectly preserved, but our lens shifts.

997
00:46:43,080 --> 00:46:47,660
Okay, so if real numbers can be salvaged with a twist, it makes me wonder about going up.

998
00:46:47,940 --> 00:46:49,840
What about pushing to higher dimensions?

999
00:46:49,960 --> 00:46:50,880
What about quaternions?

1000
00:46:50,880 --> 00:46:58,200
If real numbers are 1D lines and complex numbers are 2D planes, quaternions are 4D hypercomplex numbers.

1001
00:46:58,320 --> 00:46:59,180
4D numbers, okay.

1002
00:46:59,240 --> 00:47:02,680
They have three separate imaginary axes, I, J, and K.

1003
00:47:02,900 --> 00:47:08,700
It's entirely natural to wonder, if we can go from real to complex, do we need to go to quaternions?

1004
00:47:09,160 --> 00:47:11,080
Could the universe actually be quaternionic?

1005
00:47:11,240 --> 00:47:14,320
So we test quaternions using the same graph machinery from earlier.

1006
00:47:14,560 --> 00:47:16,620
We build Escher stairs out of quaternions.

1007
00:47:16,620 --> 00:47:21,680
We could, but the text notes a sudden, fascinating collapse in the math at higher dimensions.

1008
00:47:22,340 --> 00:47:29,300
Remember how real numbers safely handled a single matrix at D equals 2 and only failed when we built a triangle loop?

1009
